{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/spec-build/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/spec-build/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: spec-build\ndescription: Build the active spec one task at a time. Reads tasks.md first and reports the next task, writes a failing test named with the claim before the code when a task changes behavior, ticks a task only when its claims are proven, asks before touching a file the plan doesn't list, and handles a change of course mid-spec by updating the right file and rechecking every task. Use when a user asks to build, implement, continue, resume or pick up work on a spec, runs spec-build, says they are back on it, or asks where the work stands or what is next. Also use when the user changes their mind about a spec that is being built.\n---\n\n# spec-build\n\nOne task at a time, test first, in scope, and resumable by anyone.\n\n## Process\n\n1. Load docs-conventions. `references/tasks.md`, `references/plan.md` and `references/spec.md` apply.\n2. Find the active spec: the folder under the docs path's `specs/` whose slug matches the current branch. With no match, say so and ask which spec to build, or whether to start one.\n3. Read `tasks.md` first. If it doesn't exist, the kind skipped planning: create it from the template with one task per claim in the spec; for a fix that restores a claim, one task naming that claim; for a fix with no claim at all, one task with a `check:`. Add this session's line above the tasks: the date, the next unticked task, and the gist of the user's first message, never a verdict. Then report the next unticked task and any note or redo under it, before doing anything else, so nobody has to re-explain.\n4. If `spec.md` says `status: draft`, set it to `building`.\n5. Work the next unticked task only. When it names claims, write the failing test first, named with the claim reference such as `keeps one entry [wishlist/save-item#no-duplicates]`, run it and see it fail, then write the code and run it again. When it names a `check:`, do the check. Run the project's test command from `architecture/stack.md`.\n6. Stay in scope. Touch only files `plan.md` lists under Files, or, with no plan, the files the task names. Before touching any other file, stop and ask: \"This file isn't in the plan. Add it?\" When the user says yes, add it to `plan.md` with its one-line reason, then continue.\n7. Tick the task only when every claim it names has a passing test, or its check holds. Under the task, at most two lines: `- note:` for what blocked, what was tried and dropped, and why; `- redo:` when a task is reopened. Then move to the next task, or stop when the user stops.\n8. When the user changes course mid-spec, don't restart. Update the file the change belongs to: `spec.md` for why or what, `plan.md` for how, `reference/` for details. If Why or What changes changed, remove the `approved` line; the owner's new go writes it back. Then recheck every task against the updated files and mark each keep, redo, new or drop with a one-line reason; write one line above the tasks, `Rechecked <date> after: <the change>. T2 redo, T4 new, T5 dropped.`; untick a redo with a `redo:` line; remove a dropped task; add new ones with fresh numbers. Show the list and wait for an OK. Build no affected task before that OK, nor, when `spec.md` changed, before the owner's new go.\n9. When a change contradicts Not doing or grows the scope, don't absorb it. Propose a follow-up spec: \"That's outside this spec. Start a separate spec for it?\"\n\n## Verification\n\n- `tasks.md` has this session's line, and a ticked task has a passing test per claim it names.\n- Every test added carries a claim reference in its name.\n- `git status` shows no changed file outside `plan.md`'s Files list, other than ones the user added to it.\n- After a change of course, `tasks.md` has a Rechecked line and `spec.md` has no `approved` line if Why or What changes changed.\n\n## Rationalizations and red flags\n\n| Excuse | Rule |\n| --- | --- |\n| \"The test would be trivial, I'll write the code first.\" | The failing test is the proof the task asks for. Write it first, watch it fail. |\n| \"It's one line in a helper, no need to ask.\" | A file outside the plan is asked about, every time. |\n| \"The tests pass locally, mostly. Tick it.\" | Tick only on a clean run of the project's test command. |\n| \"The change is small, no need to touch spec.md.\" | A change to what gets built is a change to the spec, and the owner says go again. |\n| \"I'll just add the extra feature while I'm here.\" | Scope growth becomes a follow-up spec. |\n| \"I remember where we were, no need to read tasks.md.\" | Read it first. The next session may not be you. |\n\n## Fallbacks\n\n- No question tool: ask in plain text and wait.\n- No shell in this session: say which command to run, ask for the result, and tick only on a reported green run.\n- No test runner for the project: the check in the task names the observable result instead of a test, and it is confirmed by hand before the tick.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/spec.md && rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/plan.md && rtk read docs/glossary.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/spec.md && rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/plan.md && rtk read docs/glossary.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nid: 2026-10-13-customer-wishlist\nkind: feature\nstatus: draft\nsummary: Customers can save catalogue products to a wishlist and view or remove them.\nrequest: Customers can save items to a wishlist.\napproved: {by: Sanne, via: in session}\n---\n# Save items to a wishlist\n\n## Why\n\nCustomers can lose track of products they are interested in but are not ready to buy.\nFinding those products again takes effort, and customers may never return to complete the purchase.\nSuccess means customers return to saved products and those saved products turn into orders.\n\n## What changes\n\n### Add · wishlist/save-items\n\n- Callers can save any catalogue product to a wishlist using a supplied `customerId` string. {#save-item}\n  e.g. A caller saves a headlamp for customer-1 -> the headlamp is included in customer-1's wishlist.\n\n- Callers use one wishlist per `customerId` without signing in or creating a customer record. {#customer-id-wishlist}\n  e.g. A caller saves a headlamp and then a tent for customer-1 without signing in -> both products belong to the same customer-1 wishlist.\n\n- Callers save products without quantities. {#product-only-items}\n  e.g. A caller saves a headlamp -> the saved item represents the headlamp product without a quantity.\n\n- Callers receive an error when saving a product ID that is absent from the catalogue. {#reject-unknown-product}\n  e.g. A caller tries to save a product ID that the catalogue does not contain -> saving fails with an error.\n\n- Callers leave the wishlist unchanged when saving a product already on it, retaining one entry and its original save moment. {#preserve-first-save}\n  e.g. A caller saves a headlamp at 10:00, a tent at 10:05 and the headlamp again at 10:10 -> the headlamp remains one entry saved at 10:00, behind the tent.\n\n### Add · wishlist/view-items\n\n- Callers see only the saved products associated with the `customerId` they supply. {#isolate-wishlists}\n  e.g. Customer-1 has saved a headlamp and customer-2 has saved a tent -> viewing customer-2's wishlist shows only the tent.\n\n- Callers see each saved product with its name and the price the catalogue currently holds. {#current-product-details}\n  e.g. A headlamp was saved when its catalogue price was 3000 cents, and the catalogue now lists it at 3500 cents -> the wishlist shows the headlamp's name and 3500 cents.\n\n- Callers see saved products ordered from most recently saved to least recently saved. {#newest-first}\n  e.g. A headlamp was saved at 10:00 and a tent at 10:05 -> viewing the wishlist returns the tent before the headlamp.\n\n- Callers receive an empty list when the supplied `customerId` has no saved products. {#empty-list}\n  e.g. Customer-1 has no saved products -> viewing customer-1's wishlist returns an empty list without an error.\n\n### Add · wishlist/remove-items\n\n- Callers can remove a saved product from the wishlist identified by the supplied `customerId`. {#remove-item}\n  e.g. A caller removes the headlamp saved for customer-1 -> the headlamp no longer appears in customer-1's wishlist.\n\n- Callers receive an error when removing a product that is not on the addressed wishlist. {#reject-unsaved-product}\n  e.g. Customer-1 has saved only a tent, and a caller tries to remove a headlamp from that wishlist -> removal fails with an error.\n\n- Callers leave other customers' wishlists unchanged when removing a saved product. {#isolate-removal}\n  e.g. Customer-1 and customer-2 both saved a headlamp, and a caller removes it for customer-1 -> customer-2's wishlist still contains the headlamp.\n\n## Not doing\n\n- Sign-in or a new customer entity.\n- Buying from the wishlist; that belongs to a separate spec.\n- Sharing wishlists, named lists or multiple lists per customer.\n- Price or stock alerts.\n- Quantities or price snapshots on saved items.\n- Stock handling or behavior for products becoming unavailable or disappearing from the catalogue; the catalogue is currently fixed.\n---\nspec: 2026-10-13-customer-wishlist\n---\n## Approach\n\nImplement `src/wishlist.ts` with a module-local Map keyed by `customerId` and a per-customer Map keyed by product ID.\nExpose synchronous `saveWishlistItem`, `getWishlist` and `removeWishlistItem` operations and their types through `src/index.ts`.\nStore only product IDs and first-save timestamps; use the existing catalogue lookup to validate saves and resolve current details on reads.\nPreserve duplicate saves, sort reads newest first and keep every operation within the addressed customer's wishlist.\nUse `bun:test` claim coverage with explicit dates and distinct customers; retain the [model sketch](reference/data-model.md) and [contract draft](reference/module-design.md) as working material.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\nSanne chose process-local storage and exported module operations over persistence or a UI, as recorded in the [accepted decision](../../decisions/2026-10-13-wishlist-module-and-storage.md). This keeps the existing setup and loses wishlist state on restart.\n\n## Files\n\n```text\nsrc/\n  wishlist.ts      new: store, types and save/view/remove operations\n  index.ts         changed: re-export wishlist functions and types\ntest/\n  wishlist.test.ts new: claim coverage and public export verification\n```\n\n## Architecture to update at ship\n\n- `docs/architecture/data-model.md`, from [reference/data-model.md](reference/data-model.md).\n- `docs/architecture/boundaries.md`, from [reference/module-design.md](reference/module-design.md).\n- `docs/architecture/contracts.md`, from [reference/module-design.md](reference/module-design.md).\n- `docs/architecture/stack.md`, from [reference/module-design.md](reference/module-design.md).\n\n## Risks\n\n- A repeated save could replace the original time or move an item; `preserve-first-save` and `newest-first` prove the ordering.\n- Shared module state could mix customers; `isolate-wishlists` and `isolate-removal` prove separation, with distinct test customers preventing fixture overlap.\n- Stored product snapshots could return old prices; `current-product-details` proves that reads use the current catalogue.\n- **Customer ID**: A caller-supplied string identifying a customer. Code: `customerId`.\n- **Refund**: The monetary amount calculated for returned items. Code: `Return.refundCents`.\n- **Refunded**: The return status labelled as refunded. Code: `ReturnStatus` value `refunded`.\n- **Return**: A customer's request to send back items from an order. Code: `Return`.\n- **Return item**: A product and quantity listed in a return. Code: `ReturnItem`.\n- **Return window**: The period measured from delivery for submitting a return. Code: `RETURN_WINDOW_DAYS`.\n- **Saved item**: A product a customer has set aside for later consideration.\n- **Wishlist**: A customer's collection of saved products.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/SKILL.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/tasks.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/plan.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/spec.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/SKILL.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/tasks.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/plan.md && rtk read /Users/yousefallaban/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/references/spec.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: docs-conventions\ndescription: The file charter and the claim format for a project's memory in docs/. Load before writing or changing any file under docs/, the index, glossary, behavior, architecture, decisions, specs, plans, tasks or reference files, and before editing the project-memory block in AGENTS.md. It says which file holds what, what each never contains, where misplaced content goes, and how a claim is written.\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# Docs conventions\n\nProject memory lives under the docs path the project-memory block in AGENTS.md names, `docs/` unless the block says otherwise. Every file has one job. Content that matches a file's \"never contains\" column goes to the file the charter names for it.\n\n## Process\n\n1. Find the file's row in the charter. Read its reference under `references/` for the sections and their rules, and start from its template under `assets/`.\n2. Write only what the file's job covers. Move anything else to the file the row names.\n3. Remove every `<!-- -->` guidance comment. Delete an optional section that would be empty. Never write \"None\", \"TBD\" or a line saying the content is still to come: a required section that is not settled yet keeps its heading and nothing under it. Keep heading names and their order exactly as the template has them.\n4. Keep the frontmatter keys the template has, with no comments in the frontmatter. A quoted slot such as `id: \"{{slug}}\"` is filled without the quotes, unless the value needs them to stay valid YAML.\n5. Write claims in the claim format below, 3 to 7 per capability.\n\n## The charter\n\n| File | Job | Contains | Never contains, and where that goes | Template | Reference |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `index.md` | map of the product and its docs | what it is, the domains with their owners, where things are | details of any domain: `behavior/` | `assets/index.md` | `references/index.md` |\n| `glossary.md` | shared words | term, meaning, name in code, words to avoid | rules or behavior: `behavior/` | none, one line per term | `references/glossary.md` |\n| `behavior/<domain>/<capability>.md` | what the product does now | claims with IDs, one example each | how it's built: `architecture/`; history: git; plans: `specs/` | `assets/behavior-capability.md` | `references/behavior.md` |\n| `architecture/<area>.md` | how it's built now | one area per file, created when it first has content | behavior: `behavior/`; history: git; plans: `specs/` | `assets/architecture/` | `references/architecture.md` |\n| `decisions/<date>-<slug>.md` | why a lasting choice was made | context, options, decision, consequences | step-by-step detail: `plan.md` or `reference/` | `assets/decision.md` | `references/decision.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/spec.md` | why this change, and exactly what changes | why, claims under Add, Change and Remove, not doing, open questions | how it's built: `plan.md`; progress: `tasks.md` | `assets/spec.md` | `references/spec.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/plan.md` | how this change is built | approach, files to touch, architecture to update at ship, risks | the why: `spec.md`; progress: `tasks.md` | `assets/plan.md` | `references/plan.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/tasks.md` | where the work stands | session lines, tasks with the claims that prove them, short notes | design discussion: `plan.md` or `decisions/` | `assets/tasks.md` | `references/tasks.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/reference/*` | working material for this spec | anything the spec needs, opening with `Purpose: ...` | the why, the plan, progress: the three files above | none | `references/reference.md` |\n| AGENTS.md block | the workflow and where memory lives | the managed block between the markers | project knowledge: `docs/` | `assets/agents-block.md` | `references/agents-block.md` |\n\n## The claim format\n\n```\n- <Actor> <does or sees something observable, present tense> {#claim-id}\n  e.g. <a concrete situation> -> <the observable result>\n```\n\nOne testable sentence per claim, then one concrete example. IDs are kebab-case and unique within their capability. The full reference is `domain/capability#claim-id`; tests carry it in their names, and tasks use it whenever the bare ID is not unique in the spec. Observable behavior only: \"keeps one entry\", never \"uses a unique index\". A claim in `spec.md` reads exactly as it will in `behavior/`, so shipping copies it without rewording.\n\n## Versions\n\n`index.md` carries `docs-format`. A template change applies to new files only. Shipped specs and existing files are never rewritten to a newer format.\n\n## Verification\n\n- The file has the template's frontmatter keys and headings, in the template's order, and no `<!-- -->` comment anywhere.\n- No section is empty, and \"None\" and \"TBD\" appear nowhere.\n- Every claim has an ID and an `e.g.` line, and a capability has 3 to 7 of them.\n- Nothing in the file matches its \"never contains\" column.\nPurpose: rules for `specs/<id>/tasks.md`. Template: `assets/tasks.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `spec`, the spec id.\n\nAbove the tasks, one line per session, newest last, facts only and never a verdict: `Session YYYY-MM-DD: resumed at Tn; first message: \"<gist of the user's first message>\".` After a change mid-way, one more line: `Rechecked YYYY-MM-DD after: <the change>. T2 redo, T4 new, T5 dropped.`\n\nTasks: `- [ ] T<n> <imperative task> -> <claim IDs, or \"check: <command or observable result>\">`. Each task fits in one session. Tasks cover the build only; shipping is spec-ship's and is never a task. Numbers are never reused. A claim is written as `capability#id` when its bare ID is not unique in the spec. A task is ticked only when the claims it names are proven.\n\nUnder a task, at most two lines: `- note: <blocked, tried, why>` or `- redo: <what changed>`, the task unticked again. A dropped task is removed and named in the recheck line. Anything lasting in a note moves to plan.md or a decision record.\n\nNothing else: no design discussion, no why.\nPurpose: section rules for `specs/<id>/plan.md`. Template: `assets/plan.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `spec`, the spec id.\n\nApproach: required. The shape of the solution in 3 to 8 lines. Don't restate the why.\n\nTrade-offs: optional, when there was a real choice. The options considered and why this one won. A choice that outlives the spec links to its decision record. Not every idea mentioned in passing.\n\nFiles: required. The annotated file tree: path, new or changed or deleted, one-line reason. Code, test and config files only: the docs the flow itself writes, the spec folder, `behavior/`, `architecture/` and `decisions/`, are never listed. This is the scope the review checks the diff against, and the list spec-build asks before leaving. Not line-level edits.\n\nArchitecture to update at ship: optional, when an architecture area changes. Each area file and the reference file it comes from. spec-ship updates exactly these. Not the content itself.\n\nRollout: optional, only when order matters. Migrations, flags, deploy order, backfills. Not standard deploy steps.\n\nRisks: optional. What could break and which claim or test covers it. Not generic risks.\n\nA plan past about 60 lines means the change is too big, or design detail belongs in reference/.\nPurpose: section rules for `specs/<id>/spec.md`. Template: `assets/spec.md`.\n\nFrontmatter: `id` as YYYY-MM-DD-slug; `kind`, feature or fix; `status`, draft, building or shipped; `summary`, one user-facing line reused in release notes; `request`, the sentence, issue or commits it started from; `issue`, optional, delete the line when there is none. The owner's go is `approved: {by: <owner>, via: <in session, a message link, or a PR>}`, added only when the user reports it, never inferred; git dates it. After a change to Why or What changes, remove the line; the owner's new go writes it back. The go is never a status.\n\nWhy: required. The problem, who has it, and what it costs to leave it, in 2 to 5 lines. No solution.\n\nWhat changes: required. Claims under `### Add · <domain>/<capability>`, `### Change · ...` and `### Remove · ...`, only the headings that apply, one per capability. A Change claim carries a `was:` line with the current text on main. A Remove claim carries a `reason:` line. A fix that changes no behavior writes \"No behavior change.\" and nothing else here. Claims read exactly as they will in behavior/.\n\nNot doing: required for a feature, optional for a fix. What is deliberately out, so scope can't creep silently. Not future ideas with no decision behind them.\n\nOpen questions: while drafting, only questions the owner can answer. Delete the section when empty; the owner can't say go before that. Questions the agent can answer from the code never go here.\n\nA spec past about 80 lines is two specs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/reference/module-design.md && rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/reference/data-model.md && rtk read docs/architecture/stack.md && rtk read docs/decisions/2026-10-13-wishlist-module-and-storage.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/reference/module-design.md && rtk read docs/specs/2026-10-13-customer-wishlist/reference/data-model.md && rtk read docs/architecture/stack.md && rtk read docs/decisions/2026-10-13-wishlist-module-and-storage.md'","aggregated_output":"Purpose: draft the wishlist contracts, module boundary and verification approach that inform implementation and architecture updates at ship.\n\n# Wishlist module draft\n\n## Public contracts\n\n| Export | Proposed contract | Result and errors |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `saveWishlistItem` | `(customerId: string, productId: string, now?: Date): WishlistItem` | Validate catalogue membership and save a product; default `now` to the current time. Return a copy of the new or existing saved entry. Throw `Error` for an unknown product. |\n| `getWishlist` | `(customerId: string): WishlistProduct[]` | Return the customer's saved products with current names and prices in newest-first order; return an empty list for a customer with no entries. |\n| `removeWishlistItem` | `(customerId: string, productId: string): void` | Remove the addressed customer's saved entry; throw `Error` if the entry is absent, including when that customer has no wishlist. |\n| `WishlistItem` | Exported TypeScript type | The stored product ID and first save time described in [the model sketch](data-model.md). |\n| `WishlistProduct` | Exported TypeScript type | The read projection described in [the model sketch](data-model.md). |\n\nCustomer IDs are used as supplied; do not add customer lookup, sign-in or normalization.\nThe draft does not promise exact error message text.\n\n## Module boundary\n\n- Implement the store, types and synchronous operations in `src/wishlist.ts`, using the existing catalogue lookup from `src/products.ts` and JavaScript built-ins.\n- Re-export the public functions and types through `src/index.ts` using that file's existing export convention.\n- Put the claim tests in `test/wishlist.test.ts`. The store remains private; tests use distinct customer IDs rather than adding a public reset API.\n- Use the existing TypeScript and Bun setup. No new packages, configuration, database, UI or changes to the catalogue module are planned.\n\n## Verification design\n\n- Name behavioral tests with full references such as `wishlist/save-items#save-item`; cover all 12 claims in the approved spec.\n- Use explicit dates for duplicate-save and ordering examples, without sleeps. Exercise save/read sequences to prove that successful saves actually remain in the customer's wishlist.\n- For `current-product-details`, arrange for a catalogue price to change between save and read using the existing catalogue test facilities, and restore any changed fixture or mock afterward. Do not add a production catalogue mutation API for this test.\n- Exercise two customers independently for reads and removal, including a failed removal from a customer without the saved product.\n- Verify the three functions through `src/index.ts` after adding the barrel exports, and run the complete `bun test` suite for regressions.\n\n## Architecture sources at ship\n\n- `docs/architecture/boundaries.md`: the Wishlist module, its source and test paths, catalogue dependency and public barrel.\n- `docs/architecture/contracts.md`: the implemented public contracts listed above.\n- `docs/architecture/stack.md`: the expanded TypeScript and Bun usage; retain the existing `bun test` command.\n\nKeep this draft as working material after shipping; architecture records the verified implementation.\nPurpose: retain the wishlist data model sketch as working material for implementation and the architecture update at ship.\n\n# Wishlist data model sketch\n\nThis is the proposed model for this spec, not a description of shipped code.\n\n| Value | Proposed fields or shape | Relations |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Module-local wishlist store | `Map<string, Map<string, WishlistItem>>` | The outer key is the supplied `customerId`; the inner key is `productId`, allowing one entry per product per customer. |\n| `WishlistItem` | `productId: string`, `savedAt: Date` | References a catalogue product by ID; stores its first save moment without quantity, name or price snapshots. |\n| `WishlistProduct` | `productId: string`, `name: string`, `priceCents: number`, `savedAt: Date` | A read projection combining a saved entry with current details from the existing catalogue in `src/products.ts`. |\n\n## Storage and reads\n\n- Create a customer's inner Map only after a catalogue product has been resolved successfully for the first save.\n- Copy the supplied save time on insertion. A repeated save returns the existing entry's values without changing its timestamp or position.\n- Resolve names and prices from the catalogue for each read, then order entries by descending `savedAt`. Equal timestamps retain insertion order.\n- Return fresh item values and copied dates so callers cannot mutate the stored entries through returned values or the supplied clock value.\n- Removing an entry addresses only the supplied customer's Map; an empty customer Map can be discarded.\n- An absent customer Map reads as an empty list. There is no separate customer record or wishlist ID.\n\n## Scope of the sketch\n\nThe spec leaves disappearing catalogue products and stock handling outside this change; this draft adds no behavior for those cases.\nCatalogue field access must use the existing exports and types when implementation begins; the catalogue itself is outside the change scope.\nKeep this file as working material after shipping; update `docs/architecture/data-model.md` from the implemented model instead of moving this draft there.\n---\narea: stack\n---\n# Stack\n\n## Languages and frameworks\n\n| Name | Version | Used for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| TypeScript | Not pinned | Returns functions and types in `src/returns.ts`; shared order types in `src/orders.ts` |\n| Bun | Not pinned | Running the returns tests with `bun:test` |\n\n## Commands\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Test | `bun test` |\n---\nid: 2026-10-13-wishlist-module-and-storage\nstatus: accepted\nspec: 2026-10-13-customer-wishlist\ndate: 2026-10-13\n---\n# Keep wishlists in an in-memory module\n\n## Context\n\nThe wishlist needs state associated with a caller-supplied customer ID and operations for saving, viewing and removing products.\nSanne chose in-memory storage keyed by customer ID and a module-only implementation in this planning session.\nThe approved spec introduces neither sign-in nor a customer entity.\n\n## Options\n\n- A module-local Map keyed by customer ID: chosen by Sanne to keep storage in memory like the other modules.\n- Persistent storage: would retain wishlists across restarts but introduce storage infrastructure outside the chosen scope.\n- Exported TypeScript operations: chosen by Sanne for this repository's integration surface.\n- A UI: would introduce a presentation surface outside the chosen scope.\n\n## Decision\n\nImplement wishlists in `src/wishlist.ts` with a module-local Map keyed by `customerId`, tests in `test/wishlist.test.ts` and exports from `src/index.ts`.\n\n## Consequences\n\nThe feature can use the existing TypeScript and Bun setup without a database or UI.\nWishlists disappear on process restart and are independent in separate processes.\nThe supplied customer ID selects a wishlist; this module does not establish a caller's identity.\nA future persistence or presentation change needs a new decision and its own spec.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
